The algebra checks out. The key complication is bibliographic: the 2021 paper itself still tabulates 59–64 at \((8,1,2)\), so I cannot honestly attribute your 60 bound to the sources I could verify.
codex
1. No verified prior-art match.

Your inequality is valid, but I did not find it in the sources inspected. Do not cite van Wee (1988) as the \(\mu=2\) result: that paper treats ordinary coverings \(K(n,R)\), and its advertised even-\(n\), \(R=1\) consequence is \(K(n,1)\ge 2^n/n\), not multiple coverings. It is fair to call your proof “in the style of the excess/parity method,” but not to claim van Wee proved this specialization. [van Wee, *Improved Sphere Bounds on the Covering Radius of Codes*](https://pure.tue.nl/ws/files/1995874/353803.pdf)

The bound is
\[
K(n,1,2)\ge
\left\lceil\frac{3\cdot 2^{n+1}}{3n+2}\right\rceil
\qquad(n\text{ even}),
\]
hence 20 for \(n=6\) and 60 for \(n=8\).

2. The two cited papers:

- Hämäläinen–Honkala–Kaikkonen–Litsyn (1993) definitely studies \(K(n,r,\mu)\) and provides a small-parameter table, but I could not access its body. I therefore cannot responsibly state that it contains this general formula, nor assign a theorem number or its exact \(n=6,8\) specializations. Its public abstract alone is insufficient. [Publisher record](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01388486)
- Seuranen (2007) is not a general van-Wee-type theorem paper: its abstract says it improves 57 individual lower bounds by integer programming and exhaustive search. It is not a source for your displayed all-even-\(n\) bound. I cannot confirm its table entries from the inaccessible full text. [Publisher record](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10623-007-9089-y)

3. *Covering Codes* (1997), Chapter 14:

It certainly covers multiple coverings, and later work cites it for the definition and for perfect multiple coverings. I could not inspect the chapter’s lower-bound subsection itself, so I cannot say that it contains your bound. It should not be cited for this claim without checking the actual pages. [Book record](https://books.google.com/books?id=7KBYOt44sugC)

4. Later improvement to 59:

I found no later published improvement to the \(n=8,\mu=2\) lower bound. More importantly, Krotov–Potapov’s own 2021 paper explicitly states their general theorem and tabulates \(59\!-\!64\) for \(K(8,1,2)\); that table cites Seuranen as prior small-parameter work. So 60 is not recorded there. [Krotov–Potapov, Theorem 6 and table](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1902.00023)

5. Blunt verdict:

(b) is not the explanation. Your argument is correct.

The precise audit is:

- the ball-intersection count is correct;
- for even \(n\), the local sum is odd;
- its minimum subject to all \(n+1\) terms being at least 2 is \(2(n+1)+1\);
- \(s\le E\) is valid because \(g\) is nonnegative integral;
- the final rearrangement gives 60 at \(n=8\).

But I also cannot support (a): the verified 2021 record still publishes 59, not 60. The evidence therefore points to an omission in the tabulated lower-bound record, not a flaw in your proof and not a reference I can currently name. The right claim, pending direct inspection of the paywalled 1993 book/article material, is: “I have a valid elementary bound \(K(8,1,2)\ge60\); I have not found a published source for it.”
